```
Step 1 — Understand (2-3 min)
  ✓ Read problem twice
  ✓ Identify input/output types
  ✓ State constraints (size, values, duplicates?)
  ✓ Ask: "Can I modify input? What if input is empty?"

Step 2 — Examples (2-3 min)
  ✓ Work through given example manually
  ✓ Think of edge cases: empty, single element, all same, sorted, reversed
  ✓ Make sure you understand what the output MEANS

Step 3 — Approach (3-5 min)
  ✓ Start with brute force (even if you know better)
  ✓ Identify the pattern
  ✓ State your optimized approach before coding
  ✓ Discuss time/space complexity of your approach
  ✓ Get approval from interviewer before coding

Step 4 — Code (10-15 min)
  ✓ Write clean, readable code
  ✓ Use meaningful variable names
  ✓ Add brief comments for tricky parts
  ✓ Handle edge cases explicitly

Step 5 — Test (3-5 min)
  ✓ Trace through your given example
  ✓ Try edge cases (empty, single, max size)
  ✓ Look for off-by-one errors, null checks
  ✓ State time and space complexity

Step 6 — Optimize (if time allows)
  ✓ "Can we do better in space?"
  ✓ "Is there an O(n) solution vs O(n log n)?"
```

